An `mdp` payload is a **sub-document**: it is parsed and run through the *same*
chain as the page, so every enabled plugin reaches inside it — not just
Markdown. The payload becomes a fragment prop, and each renderer decides how to
carry a node list (Solid: `(<>{…}</>)`).

{{{mdp[card-body]
## Payload heading

A [[Nested Wiki]] link, a citation [^book "hlm. 45"] and inline math $x^2$
all resolve against the page's own state.

The image head is one node now (§7.1): the extras carry what the retired marker
run used to (`width`, `loading`), and the text after it is content.

![Chart](https://example.com/chart.png)@@figureX{.wide, width: "400", loading: "lazy"} A caption from the payload.

| Name | Value |
| ---- | ----: |
| a    |     1 |
}}}

@[Card](/docs)@@anchorA{body: $card-body}

The inline form drops the payload's own `<p>` wrapper, so a payload written on
the head line contributes inline nodes only:

{{{mdp[card-note] See [[Wireless]] and [^book]. }}}

@[Note](/docs)@@anchorA{note: $card-note}
